{
  "name": "Primary Bathroom Addition Timeline: What to Expect from Planning to Final Sign-Off",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1: Check your existing plumbing lines and main sewer routing",
      "text": "Walk through your home's current drain layout and main water line with an experienced builder. You want to verify that your sewer line has adequate slope and capacity before drawing up fixture locations. Having clear notes on your water pressure and main stack position ensures your layout works smoothly from the start.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2: Establish the room layout and fixture clearances",
      "text": "Sketch out the footprint for the vanity, curbless shower, soaking tub, and toilet room with practical spacing in mind. Ensure the draft accounts for local fixture clearances and door swings so the space stays accessible and comfortable. Once the preliminary floor plan feels natural, finalize the exact rough-in locations.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3: Secure building and specialized trade permits",
      "text": "Submit architectural and structural drawings to your city building department alongside any required sanitary district approvals. Your application must spell out plumbing, electrical, and mechanical plans clearly to prevent city plan-check delays. A complete submission pack means reviews move ahead without unnecessary revisions.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4: Complete framing, utility rough-ins, and ventilation runs",
      "text": "Frame out the new exterior walls or floor alterations, then route the dedicated drain lines, hot and cold supply pipes, exhaust ducting, and electrical circuits. Dedicated high-capacity ventilation and balanced lighting circuits should be fully set in place during this phase. Everything stays open and visible for code inspection.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 5: Schedule rough trade inspections before closing the walls",
      "text": "Call in the local building inspectors to evaluate the framing, plumbing pressure tests, and electrical rough-ins before installing any insulation or cement board. Never cover utility runs prematurely, as inspectors must verify slope, venting, and wire sizing. Once official sign-off is granted, you can safely proceed to waterproofing.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 6: Apply shower waterproofing and install durable finishes",
      "text": "Install robust shower pans, waterproof membranes, and wall boards, followed by precise tile installation, cabinetry, and countertops. Test every wet zone thoroughly before setting the final plumbing fixtures and glass enclosures. Complete the trim, paint, and final electrical fixtures with care.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 6
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 7: Walk through the final inspection and complete the punch list",
      "text": "Arrange for the building inspector to issue the final sign-off, confirming all plumbing fixtures, mechanical vents, and GFCI outlets meet local building standards. Walk the finished bathroom with your contractor to test water flow, inspect caulking, and ensure every detail matches your standards. What to Do Next If you are planning to add a primary bathroom in Albany, Berkeley, Oakland, or anywhere across the East Bay, Hometelligent Inc. can handle the entire design-build process. Contact our team at 925-383-8163 to arrange a consultation and review your site feasibility.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 7
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "speakable": {
    "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
    "cssSelector": [
      "h1",
      ".howto-step",
      "[itemprop=\"step\"]"
    ]
  },
  "totalTime": "PT30M",
